1
00:00:06,020 --> 00:00:08,090
Welcome back, everybody.

2
00:00:08,090 --> 00:00:11,690
This is going to be the last
of the more technical talks.

3
00:00:11,690 --> 00:00:13,790
We've done introduction
to Julia, introduction

4
00:00:13,790 --> 00:00:15,780
to differential
equations and plotting.

5
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:18,170
And this is essentially
introduction to Neuroblox

6
00:00:18,170 --> 00:00:19,530
from a technical point of view.

7
00:00:19,530 --> 00:00:22,340
So you will get familiar
with the structure

8
00:00:22,340 --> 00:00:26,360
of the code in Neuroblox and how
you can contribute your own code

9
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:29,780
to Neuroblox, writing your own
blox and your own connection

10
00:00:29,780 --> 00:00:31,340
rules.

11
00:00:31,340 --> 00:00:33,440
And from the next
session, so tomorrow

12
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:36,190
morning, we will get more
into the neuroscience.

13
00:00:36,190 --> 00:00:38,690
I'm going to use all these tools
from the technical sessions

14
00:00:38,690 --> 00:00:43,720
to do computational
neuroscience in Neuroblox.

15
00:00:43,720 --> 00:00:48,080
So what we will go
through is essentially

16
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:52,470
the structure of Neuroblox
and of its design concepts,

17
00:00:52,470 --> 00:00:55,100
which the main
ones are the blox.

18
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:56,490
You know blox are
the components.

19
00:00:56,490 --> 00:00:59,480
And you've seen them
as icons in the GUI.

20
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:01,940
And now, you will see
how they look in code.

21
00:01:01,940 --> 00:01:04,610
And also the connection rules.

22
00:01:04,610 --> 00:01:08,700
The connections are essentially
the edges of the graph

23
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:10,915
if the blox are the nodes.

24
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:16,400
And these two things
together essentially

25
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:18,540
are the core linear blox.

26
00:01:18,540 --> 00:01:21,380
Exactly how I said before, the
types and multiple dispatch

27
00:01:21,380 --> 00:01:23,540
are the core in Julia.

28
00:01:23,540 --> 00:01:28,970
There's not one-to-one mapping
because we will see custom types

29
00:01:28,970 --> 00:01:31,380
in both blox and connections.

30
00:01:31,380 --> 00:01:34,300
But multiple dispatch is mainly
exploited by connection rules.

31
00:01:37,160 --> 00:01:41,430
So yeah, as I said, we have the
types and multiple dispatch.

32
00:01:41,430 --> 00:01:48,680
And when I talk about types, I
mean in particular type trees.

33
00:01:48,680 --> 00:01:51,650
Much like yesterday
or on the exercises

34
00:01:51,650 --> 00:01:53,030
and on the
presentation, where we

35
00:01:53,030 --> 00:01:57,080
show how number,
as a type in Julia,

36
00:01:57,080 --> 00:02:01,700
is a super type above integers
and above floating point--

37
00:02:01,700 --> 00:02:03,240
numbers of any precision.

38
00:02:03,240 --> 00:02:05,360
So that is a small type tree.

39
00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:08,100
Exactly like that in Julia,
we have-- sorry, in Neuroblox,

40
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:10,680
we have type trees
of all of our blox.

41
00:02:10,680 --> 00:02:13,580
And every node in
that tree could

42
00:02:13,580 --> 00:02:17,660
be either a primitive block
that is neurons or neuron

43
00:02:17,660 --> 00:02:22,370
masses, blox that contain
dynamics in them immediately.

44
00:02:22,370 --> 00:02:25,730
They could be composite
types, so composite blox

45
00:02:25,730 --> 00:02:29,090
that are blox that
contain neurons or neuron

46
00:02:29,090 --> 00:02:29,940
masses in them.

47
00:02:29,940 --> 00:02:31,820
So they could represent
different circuits.

48
00:02:31,820 --> 00:02:34,550
And we also have some
abstract types where,

49
00:02:34,550 --> 00:02:37,310
as you saw in the
exercises yesterday,

50
00:02:37,310 --> 00:02:40,950
you cannot instantiate an
object that is an abstract type.

51
00:02:40,950 --> 00:02:44,060
But abstract types
help us [? as ?] some

52
00:02:44,060 --> 00:02:48,320
hubs in the type tree that
branch out to multiple nodes

53
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:51,798
and helps us to organize
the type tree better,

54
00:02:51,798 --> 00:02:53,340
and they help with
multiple dispatch.

55
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:55,760
You will see how.

56
00:02:55,760 --> 00:02:59,720
So this is a partial
view of the type tree

57
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:02,585
that we have in Neuroblox.

58
00:03:02,585 --> 00:03:06,470
Above all, the most general
type that we have is this type

59
00:03:06,470 --> 00:03:09,680
called abstract blox.

60
00:03:09,680 --> 00:03:12,690
And everything else, every
other blox is a subtype of this.

61
00:03:12,690 --> 00:03:15,470
And here, I'm showing you
four main categories that

62
00:03:15,470 --> 00:03:17,750
are subtypes of abstract blox.

63
00:03:17,750 --> 00:03:22,830
We have neurons, neural masses,
stimuli, and observer blox.

64
00:03:22,830 --> 00:03:26,780
And then if we follow the
tree down towards the leaves,

65
00:03:26,780 --> 00:03:28,560
neuron can be split into--

66
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:30,210
and again, this is
only a partial view.

67
00:03:30,210 --> 00:03:34,460
But we have, for instance,
abstract [INAUDIBLE] neuroblox,

68
00:03:34,460 --> 00:03:38,600
where we put all of our
bloxes that are explicitly

69
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:40,855
excitatory in their dynamics.

70
00:03:40,855 --> 00:03:43,040
And likewise,
abstract inhibitory

71
00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:48,920
are innate neuroblox for
inhibitory neuron types.

72
00:03:48,920 --> 00:03:53,320
But we also have neurons that
are just subtypes of neuron.

73
00:03:53,320 --> 00:03:56,640
There's no other
node underneath.

74
00:03:56,640 --> 00:04:00,800
So like a LIF neuron, that's a
leaky integrate and fire neuron,

75
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:04,790
similar to the generalized
integrate and fire that you saw

76
00:04:04,790 --> 00:04:07,130
in the exercise in the morning.

77
00:04:07,130 --> 00:04:08,430
That just falls under neuron.

78
00:04:08,430 --> 00:04:10,633
There's no other category here.

79
00:04:10,633 --> 00:04:12,050
And because the
neuron can be used

80
00:04:12,050 --> 00:04:14,420
for both excitatory and
inhibitory dynamics,

81
00:04:14,420 --> 00:04:18,329
it doesn't fall in any
other of the two categories.

82
00:04:18,329 --> 00:04:21,690
But if we keep following
the abstract categories,

83
00:04:21,690 --> 00:04:24,440
because in theory, every
abstract category is not

84
00:04:24,440 --> 00:04:26,750
a leaf in the type
tree, it has to be

85
00:04:26,750 --> 00:04:28,080
followed by something else.

86
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:31,550
Otherwise, there's
no function for it.

87
00:04:31,550 --> 00:04:33,480
We have Hodgkin-Huxley neurons.

88
00:04:33,480 --> 00:04:35,850
That's the H-H
always in your blox.

89
00:04:35,850 --> 00:04:39,920
So have a Hodgkin-Huxley
neuron that is explicitly

90
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:42,770
designed to be excitatory
and, likewise, one

91
00:04:42,770 --> 00:04:44,240
that is inhibitory.

92
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:48,390
And for neural masses, these
are also split into multiple.

93
00:04:48,390 --> 00:04:50,450
If I recall correctly,
there is no abstract type

94
00:04:50,450 --> 00:04:51,780
beneath neural mass.

95
00:04:51,780 --> 00:04:54,230
So we only have
different blox that

96
00:04:54,230 --> 00:04:57,860
are neural mass blox
from the literature,

97
00:04:57,860 --> 00:05:01,520
like Wilson-Cowan or
Lauter-Breakspear.

98
00:05:01,520 --> 00:05:03,950
And in the stimulus
category, we have

99
00:05:03,950 --> 00:05:08,730
anything that is an
external stimulus into blox.

100
00:05:08,730 --> 00:05:10,122
That can be
[INAUDIBLE] stimulus.

101
00:05:10,122 --> 00:05:11,830
As you saw in the
category learning task,

102
00:05:11,830 --> 00:05:16,450
we can provide images
that are pixel by pixel

103
00:05:16,450 --> 00:05:18,600
matched to blox as an input.

104
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:20,970
Or we have blox like
the Poisson spike train

105
00:05:20,970 --> 00:05:23,230
that does exactly what it says.

106
00:05:23,230 --> 00:05:26,070
It just generates spikes
with a Poisson process

107
00:05:26,070 --> 00:05:28,380
that go into your neuron.

108
00:05:28,380 --> 00:05:30,990
And observer blox,
we'll see some of them

109
00:05:30,990 --> 00:05:34,230
on Friday when we
talk about DCM.

110
00:05:34,230 --> 00:05:36,210
So these are like
measurement blox.

111
00:05:36,210 --> 00:05:39,280
These are blox that represent
some experimental measure.

112
00:05:39,280 --> 00:05:42,900
So if you attach that on top
of a block with dynamics,

113
00:05:42,900 --> 00:05:47,280
you can transform some variables
in that blox-- for instance,

114
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:48,060
its voltage--

115
00:05:48,060 --> 00:05:51,592
into something that is actually
measurable in an experiment--

116
00:05:51,592 --> 00:05:52,925
for instance, [? volt ?] signal.

117
00:05:55,560 --> 00:05:59,070
So here, I just want to show
you a couple of examples

118
00:05:59,070 --> 00:06:01,890
of blox definitions so
you can see how they

119
00:06:01,890 --> 00:06:03,640
look like in the source code.

120
00:06:03,640 --> 00:06:07,040
And you will design some
of these yourselves.

121
00:06:07,040 --> 00:06:09,320
So this is a
Wilson-Cowan, which is

122
00:06:09,320 --> 00:06:12,170
a subtype of neural
mass block using

123
00:06:12,170 --> 00:06:14,430
the syntax for subtyping here.

124
00:06:14,430 --> 00:06:17,850
And as you will see
shortly in the exercises,

125
00:06:17,850 --> 00:06:20,540
the necessary
fields for any blox

126
00:06:20,540 --> 00:06:23,520
are two-- essentially, the
system and the namespace.

127
00:06:23,520 --> 00:06:25,080
The params here is optional.

128
00:06:25,080 --> 00:06:29,550
We have it for some convenience
internally in the code,

129
00:06:29,550 --> 00:06:32,360
but you don't really
need this params.

130
00:06:32,360 --> 00:06:35,930
And the system field is going to
hold the system of differential

131
00:06:35,930 --> 00:06:38,960
equations.

132
00:06:38,960 --> 00:06:41,030
We'll talk more about
namespace tomorrow

133
00:06:41,030 --> 00:06:42,590
when we talk about
circuits, but this

134
00:06:42,590 --> 00:06:46,340
is essentially to designate
where exactly in the hierarchy

135
00:06:46,340 --> 00:06:48,690
of blox is we are right now.

136
00:06:48,690 --> 00:06:51,470
Because if, for
instance, these blox

137
00:06:51,470 --> 00:06:54,290
is a final blox in our
graph, then the namespace

138
00:06:54,290 --> 00:06:57,300
is only the name
of the final model.

139
00:06:57,300 --> 00:07:00,050
But if these blox is
hidden inside another blox

140
00:07:00,050 --> 00:07:02,090
and another blox,
then this namespace

141
00:07:02,090 --> 00:07:04,250
carries that information,
so we know exactly where

142
00:07:04,250 --> 00:07:08,390
we are with each object.

143
00:07:08,390 --> 00:07:11,060
And when you define a
function, which is essentially

144
00:07:11,060 --> 00:07:14,630
a constructor for our blox,
so we need to tell Julia,

145
00:07:14,630 --> 00:07:18,180
essentially, how to construct
an object called Wilson-Cowan.

146
00:07:18,180 --> 00:07:19,920
So this is what this
function is doing.

147
00:07:19,920 --> 00:07:22,655
And this is an
internal constructor.

148
00:07:22,655 --> 00:07:24,530
And it's called internal
because the function

149
00:07:24,530 --> 00:07:30,125
definition, as you can see, is
inside the structure definition.

150
00:07:30,125 --> 00:07:33,300
And we do it like
this for convenience.

151
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:35,390
And also if you define
an internal constructor,

152
00:07:35,390 --> 00:07:37,310
then that's the
default one, and that's

153
00:07:37,310 --> 00:07:40,700
the only internal constructor
that will be used.

154
00:07:40,700 --> 00:07:44,690
So for instance, you cannot
now define a Wilson-Cowan block

155
00:07:44,690 --> 00:07:47,660
by providing params, and system,
and namespace because we're not

156
00:07:47,660 --> 00:07:52,520
expecting everybody to provide
these three fields as inputs

157
00:07:52,520 --> 00:07:53,730
to a constructor.

158
00:07:53,730 --> 00:07:56,720
We expect somebody to
provide in all the parameters

159
00:07:56,720 --> 00:07:58,590
that the Wilson-Cowan block has.

160
00:07:58,590 --> 00:08:01,200
And you can see these are
all keyword arguments.

161
00:08:01,200 --> 00:08:05,240
And we always stick to keyword
arguments for convenience.

162
00:08:05,240 --> 00:08:09,335
You have to explicitly type the
parameter name and the value

163
00:08:09,335 --> 00:08:11,460
so you know exactly which
parameter you're setting,

164
00:08:11,460 --> 00:08:12,650
what value.

165
00:08:12,650 --> 00:08:16,010
So you supply all
these parameters

166
00:08:16,010 --> 00:08:17,760
that are relevant to your blox.

167
00:08:17,760 --> 00:08:22,220
And then you would
define a set of variables

168
00:08:22,220 --> 00:08:26,030
exactly as you did in the
morning session and also

169
00:08:26,030 --> 00:08:27,930
a set of parameters if needed.

170
00:08:27,930 --> 00:08:29,990
This is done through
parameter scoping here.

171
00:08:29,990 --> 00:08:33,590
Parameter scoping is some
blox function internally

172
00:08:33,590 --> 00:08:36,950
to figure out if your parameters
are shared with other blox.

173
00:08:36,950 --> 00:08:38,720
So you can do that as well.

174
00:08:38,720 --> 00:08:41,610
Say that you have 500 of
these Wilson-Cowan blox,

175
00:08:41,610 --> 00:08:49,320
but you want all of them to
have one common tau v parameter.

176
00:08:49,320 --> 00:08:52,210
This is handled internally by
this parameter scoping function.

177
00:08:54,740 --> 00:08:56,340
The equations--
exactly as before,

178
00:08:56,340 --> 00:08:59,730
you type them out symbolically
using modeling toolkit syntax.

179
00:08:59,730 --> 00:09:02,390
And finally, you just
define your system.

180
00:09:02,390 --> 00:09:05,810
And you supply that
system the namespace

181
00:09:05,810 --> 00:09:12,050
and, optionally, the
parameters in your call

182
00:09:12,050 --> 00:09:13,110
to this new function.

183
00:09:13,110 --> 00:09:15,920
And this new function
is a special function

184
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:19,610
for any internal constructor
that basically outputs

185
00:09:19,610 --> 00:09:22,610
an object of type Wilson-Cowan.

186
00:09:22,610 --> 00:09:24,860
Because this function is a
constructor, so at the end,

187
00:09:24,860 --> 00:09:29,360
it's going to construct an
object of this type for us.

188
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:33,230
And likewise, this is the
definition of an alive neuron--

189
00:09:33,230 --> 00:09:35,070
a leaky integrate
and fire neuron.

190
00:09:35,070 --> 00:09:39,230
As you can see, this time,
it's a subtype of neuron.

191
00:09:39,230 --> 00:09:41,250
It's important to
designate this.

192
00:09:41,250 --> 00:09:43,820
And again, as an
input, it takes in all

193
00:09:43,820 --> 00:09:47,990
of the relevant
parameters for the model.

194
00:09:47,990 --> 00:09:52,100
It passes those values to
symbolic parameters here.

195
00:09:52,100 --> 00:09:55,880
And it defines its variables
with some default values,

196
00:09:55,880 --> 00:09:56,880
equations.

197
00:09:56,880 --> 00:09:59,340
And the extra thing
here, of course,

198
00:09:59,340 --> 00:10:03,260
is the spiking, because this
is an integrate and fire

199
00:10:03,260 --> 00:10:05,742
neuron, so we need to
do the spiking manually

200
00:10:05,742 --> 00:10:07,700
exactly how you did with
the [INAUDIBLE] neuron

201
00:10:07,700 --> 00:10:08,550
in the morning.

202
00:10:08,550 --> 00:10:11,120
So in our system
definition here,

203
00:10:11,120 --> 00:10:15,740
we just pass a discrete
event, the spiking event.

204
00:10:15,740 --> 00:10:19,020
And yeah, again, the
same call to new.

205
00:10:19,020 --> 00:10:24,440
This is basically all you need
to write a new [? blox. ?]

206
00:10:24,440 --> 00:10:27,620
Now, because neuron
already comes

207
00:10:27,620 --> 00:10:31,730
with a lot of blox
already implemented--

208
00:10:31,730 --> 00:10:34,700
of course, one way
of looking at them

209
00:10:34,700 --> 00:10:36,810
would be looking directly
at the source code.

210
00:10:36,810 --> 00:10:39,300
If you want to do
that, that's fine.

211
00:10:39,300 --> 00:10:42,290
But I would say an easier way
and a more user-friendly way

212
00:10:42,290 --> 00:10:44,380
is to go through help.

213
00:10:44,380 --> 00:10:46,855
And this is a general
Julia feature.

214
00:10:46,855 --> 00:10:52,370
If you are in a Julia session,
and you type in the question

215
00:10:52,370 --> 00:10:55,100
mark, then it jumps
from the normal Julia

216
00:10:55,100 --> 00:10:56,320
session to this help.

217
00:10:58,850 --> 00:11:01,680
If you type in any
Julia type here,

218
00:11:01,680 --> 00:11:03,920
it's going to give you
essentially the docstring

219
00:11:03,920 --> 00:11:05,450
of that type, which
is essentially

220
00:11:05,450 --> 00:11:07,500
a short documentation
for that type.

221
00:11:07,500 --> 00:11:10,790
And we have written
documentation

222
00:11:10,790 --> 00:11:13,140
like this for our blox.

223
00:11:13,140 --> 00:11:16,130
So if you type, for instance,
help, question mark,

224
00:11:16,130 --> 00:11:18,050
[? alive ?] neuron,
then the output

225
00:11:18,050 --> 00:11:22,250
is this docstring,
essentially, which

226
00:11:22,250 --> 00:11:26,930
describes what this block is,
what these parameters are,

227
00:11:26,930 --> 00:11:29,720
and how you can construct it.

228
00:11:29,720 --> 00:11:32,180
So then there's also a
reference because these blox

229
00:11:32,180 --> 00:11:33,560
comes from the literature.

230
00:11:33,560 --> 00:11:36,110
It's implemented
based on a paper.

231
00:11:36,110 --> 00:11:38,040
So you can know more about this.

232
00:11:38,040 --> 00:11:41,930
So you can know more about
how to use it in your code.

233
00:11:41,930 --> 00:11:45,020
Now, moving on to
connections, as I said,

234
00:11:45,020 --> 00:11:47,840
both blox and connections
have custom types.

235
00:11:47,840 --> 00:11:49,790
And the custom type
for connections

236
00:11:49,790 --> 00:11:54,050
is this connector object.

237
00:11:54,050 --> 00:11:58,340
Essentially, every
connection-- every edge

238
00:11:58,340 --> 00:12:00,650
in our graph, every
connection between two blox

239
00:12:00,650 --> 00:12:02,250
will generate a
connector object.

240
00:12:02,250 --> 00:12:04,490
And that object will
hold all information

241
00:12:04,490 --> 00:12:09,050
about how these two blox
should talk to one another.

242
00:12:09,050 --> 00:12:12,540
And in order to create
these connectors,

243
00:12:12,540 --> 00:12:15,090
we also have
constructor functions.

244
00:12:15,090 --> 00:12:18,080
And these constructors rely on
multiple dispatch, essentially,

245
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:19,850
to do so.

246
00:12:19,850 --> 00:12:24,710
And the multiple dispatch
relies on the type

247
00:12:24,710 --> 00:12:29,065
of the source blox, the type
of the destination blox.

248
00:12:29,065 --> 00:12:30,530
So we can have
different rules that

249
00:12:30,530 --> 00:12:33,650
are different combinations of a
source block and a destination

250
00:12:33,650 --> 00:12:34,370
blox.

251
00:12:34,370 --> 00:12:37,700
And of course, some
rules are reasonable,

252
00:12:37,700 --> 00:12:39,390
like connecting two
neurons together.

253
00:12:39,390 --> 00:12:41,330
There is a rule for that.

254
00:12:41,330 --> 00:12:44,930
But some rules are
not implementing

255
00:12:44,930 --> 00:12:48,750
[? Neuroblox ?] because we
never expect them to be used.

256
00:12:48,750 --> 00:12:51,980
For instance, if you want to
connect a neuron to a stimulus,

257
00:12:51,980 --> 00:12:54,093
that connection
doesn't apply, and it

258
00:12:54,093 --> 00:12:55,260
doesn't exist in your rules.

259
00:12:55,260 --> 00:12:57,950
But of course, a connection
from a stimulus to a neuron

260
00:12:57,950 --> 00:13:01,370
exists, whether that's a
stimulus, or a Poisson spike

261
00:13:01,370 --> 00:13:03,710
train, or any other.

262
00:13:03,710 --> 00:13:07,795
And now, when you define
a new blox, sometimes--

263
00:13:07,795 --> 00:13:09,420
most of the time, in
fact, it's enough.

264
00:13:09,420 --> 00:13:11,460
If you subtype it as
something-- for instance,

265
00:13:11,460 --> 00:13:14,300
you define a neuron model,
and you subtype that as neuron

266
00:13:14,300 --> 00:13:18,410
or a neural mass subtype
as neural mass block--

267
00:13:18,410 --> 00:13:21,612
that is enough for it to fall
under some connection rule.

268
00:13:21,612 --> 00:13:23,570
So even if you don't
define your own connection

269
00:13:23,570 --> 00:13:25,100
rule, and you only define a--

270
00:13:25,100 --> 00:13:26,840
sorry, a custom blox--

271
00:13:26,840 --> 00:13:29,600
then you should be able to
interface with other blox

272
00:13:29,600 --> 00:13:32,340
because we have some
generic connection rules.

273
00:13:32,340 --> 00:13:36,710
So if Julia doesn't find a
connection rule specifically

274
00:13:36,710 --> 00:13:39,980
for your blox, it's going to
fall back to the generic one.

275
00:13:39,980 --> 00:13:44,490
But of course, if you want to--
if your case is more exotic,

276
00:13:44,490 --> 00:13:46,640
or you want some
specific connection rule,

277
00:13:46,640 --> 00:13:48,420
it's easy to define your own.

278
00:13:48,420 --> 00:13:53,480
So then the generic one will
be replaced by your own.

279
00:13:53,480 --> 00:13:57,350
And what you need to
define a connection rule

280
00:13:57,350 --> 00:13:59,730
is basically essentially
summarized here.

281
00:13:59,730 --> 00:14:01,910
Here, I'm showing you
again a screenshot

282
00:14:01,910 --> 00:14:03,570
from the neural
block source code.

283
00:14:03,570 --> 00:14:07,010
And this is the most
general definition

284
00:14:07,010 --> 00:14:10,820
of a connector object that
takes in-- as you can see,

285
00:14:10,820 --> 00:14:15,060
both of the source block
is just an abstract block.

286
00:14:15,060 --> 00:14:16,350
It could be any type of block.

287
00:14:16,350 --> 00:14:19,650
And the destination blox can
be, again, abstract blox--

288
00:14:19,650 --> 00:14:21,535
anything.

289
00:14:21,535 --> 00:14:25,940
But we don't expect the user
to write connector constructors

290
00:14:25,940 --> 00:14:26,670
like this.

291
00:14:26,670 --> 00:14:29,720
We expect the user to
add their own [INAUDIBLE]

292
00:14:29,720 --> 00:14:33,013
[? pods ?] to these
functions highlighted here.

293
00:14:33,013 --> 00:14:34,430
So we have four
functions that you

294
00:14:34,430 --> 00:14:38,130
can extend with your own rules.

295
00:14:38,130 --> 00:14:41,160
So maybe the most important
one is connection rule.

296
00:14:41,160 --> 00:14:44,390
So if you want to write--

297
00:14:44,390 --> 00:14:45,920
sorry, connection
equations-- if you

298
00:14:45,920 --> 00:14:47,870
want to write your own
connection equations,

299
00:14:47,870 --> 00:14:49,115
you can import this function.

300
00:14:49,115 --> 00:14:50,990
You will see how to do
that in the exercises.

301
00:14:50,990 --> 00:14:53,490
And just write your own
function definition.

302
00:14:53,490 --> 00:14:56,550
And that adds another
dispatch for this function.

303
00:14:56,550 --> 00:15:01,640
So when actually Neuroblox
tries to connect your blox

304
00:15:01,640 --> 00:15:05,660
with another blox, and you have
connection equations defined

305
00:15:05,660 --> 00:15:08,490
for your blox, comma,
and another type of blox,

306
00:15:08,490 --> 00:15:12,350
then it's going to
call your function.

307
00:15:12,350 --> 00:15:15,410
Likewise, you can add the
connection learning rule.

308
00:15:15,410 --> 00:15:17,160
So this applies to
reinforcement learning.

309
00:15:17,160 --> 00:15:19,035
And we will talk about
reinforcement learning

310
00:15:19,035 --> 00:15:22,290
on plasticity on Thursday.

311
00:15:22,290 --> 00:15:25,340
So if you want those
symbolic weights that get

312
00:15:25,340 --> 00:15:28,250
generated every time you add
a connection-- if you want

313
00:15:28,250 --> 00:15:30,930
those weights to update based
on some plasticity rule,

314
00:15:30,930 --> 00:15:35,450
you can write that in a function
called connection learning rule

315
00:15:35,450 --> 00:15:38,430
and extend the sense
with this definition.

316
00:15:38,430 --> 00:15:40,430
Connection callbacks
can be any callbacks

317
00:15:40,430 --> 00:15:43,230
that you would like
to add to connection.

318
00:15:43,230 --> 00:15:47,602
We left this blank for
anything that you want to add.

319
00:15:47,602 --> 00:15:49,310
There are connection
spike effects where,

320
00:15:49,310 --> 00:15:53,690
essentially, specific callbacks
that apply to spiking.

321
00:15:53,690 --> 00:15:56,570
And we have this
separated for convenience

322
00:15:56,570 --> 00:15:59,600
because you will see that
connection spike effects are

323
00:15:59,600 --> 00:16:00,750
easier to write.

324
00:16:00,750 --> 00:16:05,545
And you can write them as,
when this source neuron spikes,

325
00:16:05,545 --> 00:16:08,330
apply these equations.

326
00:16:08,330 --> 00:16:14,090
So again, all you need to do to
write your own custom connection

327
00:16:14,090 --> 00:16:18,300
rule is define all or maybe
some of these four functions.

328
00:16:18,300 --> 00:16:21,770
Sometimes all you want is
some continuous equation

329
00:16:21,770 --> 00:16:23,040
between two blox.

330
00:16:23,040 --> 00:16:25,380
So it's enough to define
connection equations.

331
00:16:25,380 --> 00:16:29,060
But maybe you have neurons, like
the [INAUDIBLE] neuron, which

332
00:16:29,060 --> 00:16:31,200
has this event type spiking.

333
00:16:31,200 --> 00:16:35,253
So you want, at every event, to
affect the destination neuron.

334
00:16:35,253 --> 00:16:37,670
And so in that case, you would
extend the connection spike

335
00:16:37,670 --> 00:16:40,130
effect.

336
00:16:40,130 --> 00:16:44,335
So finally, as I said, because
we have many neurons implemented

337
00:16:44,335 --> 00:16:45,710
in [? Neuroblox, ?]
and actually,

338
00:16:45,710 --> 00:16:52,340
the library is growing, there
can be a combinatorial explosion

339
00:16:52,340 --> 00:16:57,650
of connection rules, because
many of the viable pairs of blox

340
00:16:57,650 --> 00:16:59,790
is together can have
specialized rules.

341
00:16:59,790 --> 00:17:03,560
But you, as a user, if you
want to see what rule applies

342
00:17:03,560 --> 00:17:05,839
to a couple of blox,
you can actually

343
00:17:05,839 --> 00:17:08,900
call this function, what's
called connection rule.

344
00:17:08,900 --> 00:17:10,503
Here, I just defined two bloxes.

345
00:17:10,503 --> 00:17:11,670
You will see how to do this.

346
00:17:11,670 --> 00:17:13,760
And then I call my
connection rule function

347
00:17:13,760 --> 00:17:17,869
with the source neutron first
and the destination neuron,

348
00:17:17,869 --> 00:17:21,119
or blox in general, second,
and then any keyword arguments

349
00:17:21,119 --> 00:17:24,560
because wait here is a keyword
argument that I've just

350
00:17:24,560 --> 00:17:25,890
given it a value of 1.

351
00:17:25,890 --> 00:17:27,890
And then I get a
nice printout that

352
00:17:27,890 --> 00:17:29,990
tells me what
connection am I looking

353
00:17:29,990 --> 00:17:33,260
at, what's the equation that
applies to this connection,

354
00:17:33,260 --> 00:17:35,760
and any other additional things.

355
00:17:35,760 --> 00:17:37,910
For instance, it
tells me the weights

356
00:17:37,910 --> 00:17:39,650
that have been
generated specifically

357
00:17:39,650 --> 00:17:41,090
for this connection.

358
00:17:41,090 --> 00:17:43,040
So when this connection
happens in the model,

359
00:17:43,040 --> 00:17:46,190
I'm going to get a symbolic
parameter generated that is

360
00:17:46,190 --> 00:17:50,825
called weight_w_, the name of
the source neuron, underscore,

361
00:17:50,825 --> 00:17:52,560
the name of the
destination neuron.

362
00:17:52,560 --> 00:17:55,115
And we made it so that these
weights are easy to find.

363
00:17:55,115 --> 00:17:57,240
So maybe you want to print
your weights at the end,

364
00:17:57,240 --> 00:17:58,740
especially if you're
doing learning.

365
00:17:58,740 --> 00:18:00,320
So they're easy
to find like this.

366
00:18:00,320 --> 00:18:03,530
But also this
specific connection,

367
00:18:03,530 --> 00:18:06,320
because it's a layer of neurons
and they have this event type

368
00:18:06,320 --> 00:18:09,690
spiking, also includes
some spike effects.

369
00:18:09,690 --> 00:18:11,760
And you also get a
nice print of this.

370
00:18:11,760 --> 00:18:17,270
So the print tells you that
each time LIF1 spikes, then

371
00:18:17,270 --> 00:18:20,550
it affects the
specific state on LIF2.

372
00:18:20,550 --> 00:18:24,890
So it affects the
[INAUDIBLE] of LIF2

373
00:18:24,890 --> 00:18:28,940
by incrementing it by the
symbolic weight, basically.

374
00:18:28,940 --> 00:18:32,040
But this function applies
to any combination of blox.

375
00:18:32,040 --> 00:18:34,220
So if you're
uncertain about what's

376
00:18:34,220 --> 00:18:36,410
the rule connecting these
two objects together,

377
00:18:36,410 --> 00:18:37,423
you can call this.

378
00:18:37,423 --> 00:18:39,090
And if you're unsatisfied
by the answer,

379
00:18:39,090 --> 00:18:41,660
maybe you can write
your own rule.

380
00:18:41,660 --> 00:18:45,560
And this was the
end, essentially.

381
00:18:45,560 --> 00:18:50,810
So yeah, exactly like
the morning session.

382
00:18:50,810 --> 00:18:52,490
But from now on,
it's going to be

383
00:18:52,490 --> 00:18:55,880
a single notebook per session.

384
00:18:55,880 --> 00:18:59,040
You can just navigate to blox
and connection in Neuroblox.

385
00:18:59,040 --> 00:19:01,380
This is essentially the page,
exactly like the notebook.

386
00:19:01,380 --> 00:19:05,520
And the notebook name is the
blox connections are here.

387
00:19:05,520 --> 00:19:07,080
So yeah, please
open the notebook.

